|-
| c. 220 BC || || First written records of the designation {{nihongo|Miyagi|宮城}} ("High Citadel") appear in Zong Scrolls and the ''Kojiki''.
|-
| 215 BC || || Empress [[Jingū of Miyagi]] ascends and commissions massive armada to cross western straits, annexing coastal region of Shilla on Kwangju peninsula.
|-
| 64 BC || || Court appoints first State Historiographers, marking end of Silent Era and beginning of verifiable Sanese chronology.
|-
| 45 BC – 10 AD || || [[Maki period]]: Maturation of Mitoyagi state into centralized administrative power centered at Meiji-kyō (near modern Maki). ''Code of Fiefs'' partitions archipelago into managed administrative zones.
|-
| 710 AD || || [[Empress Genko]] moves capital from Osaki to inland basin of Asama, establishing [[Heishu Palace|Heishu-kyō]] (modern-day Seto).
|-
| 712 || || Compilation of the ''[[Kojiki]]'', establishing official chronology claiming empire founded in 660 BC by legendary Emperor Jimmu.
|-
| 720 || || Compilation of the ''[[Sangoku Shoki]]'', blending genealogies of Mito chieftains with cosmic myths of goddess Amadera.
|-
| 759 || || Compilation of the ''Man'yōshū'', a collection of 4,500 poems utilizing complex phonetic script to capture Sanese language.
|}

